<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/diversification-and-portfolios">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Diversification is finance's only free lunch: <strong>spreading investments across imperfectly
    correlated assets reduces risk without reducing expected return</strong>. It works because
    assets don't all fail together — and its power depends entirely on correlation, which is why
    "own many things" and "own many different things" are different strategies. This page builds
    the idea properly: how return and risk are measured, what happens mathematically when you
    combine assets, how Markowitz turned that into portfolio construction, what CAPM says the
    market pays you for, and how a real portfolio is built and maintained.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mathematics of not losing everything at once" widget="equationStep" config={divCfg} />

  <h2>Measuring return and risk</h2>
  <p>
    Two numbers describe most of an asset's character. <strong>Expected return</strong> is the
    average outcome — the centre of the distribution. <strong>Risk</strong> is measured as
    <strong>volatility</strong>: the standard deviation of returns around that average. An asset
    returning about 7 per cent a year with 20 per cent volatility will, in a typical year, land
    anywhere between −13 and +27 — and once a decade will land far outside. Volatility also
    quietly eats return: the return you actually compound (the <em>geometric</em> return) is
    roughly the arithmetic mean minus half the variance (σ²/2). A 20 per cent volatile asset
    therefore compounds about 2 percentage points below its average return — the arithmetic of
    a 50 per cent fall needing a 100 per cent gain to catch up. This is the first reason risk
    reduction is not cosmetic: smoothing the path raises the compounded outcome even at the
    same average.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because return and risk always travel together, professionals quote them as a ratio.
    The <strong>Sharpe ratio</strong> — (return − risk-free rate) divided by volatility —
    measures how much excess return each unit of risk buys. A fund earning 9 per cent with
    18 per cent volatility scores (9 − 2) / 18 ≈ 0.39; a fund earning 7 per cent with
    10 per cent volatility scores 0.50 and is the better engine, despite the lower headline
    number. This single idea reframes every comparison: the question is never "which return
    is higher?" but "which return is higher <em>per unit of risk</em>?" Diversification is
    the main tool for raising a Sharpe ratio, because it strips out risk that carries no
    extra return — which is exactly what the ratio rewards.
  </p>

  <h2>The mathematics of combining assets</h2>
  <p>
    When you combine assets, expected returns average weighted, but risks do
    <strong>not</strong>. The portfolio's volatility depends on the correlation ρ between the
    assets. Take two assets, each with 20 per cent volatility, in a 50/50 split: with
    ρ = +1 (the same asset twice) the portfolio is 20 per cent volatile — no benefit. With
    ρ = 0 (independent drivers) the portfolio volatility falls to 20 × √0.5 ≈
    <strong>14.1 per cent</strong>. With ρ = −1 (perfect opposites) it can be engineered to
    <strong>zero</strong> — a perfect hedge. The general law for many uncorrelated assets is the
    <strong>√n rule</strong>: n independent bets of equal risk combine to risk of one divided
    by √n. One hundred independent stocks, each 30 per cent volatile, make a portfolio of
    about 3 per cent volatility. The catch is the word "independent": thirty technology stocks
    share one macro driver (rates, tech spending), so their correlations cluster near 1 and
    the √n law never fires. Diversify across <em>kinds</em> — equities, bonds, geographies,
    currencies — not within one kind.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> diversification means owning a lot. It means owning
    <em>drivers</em> that don't coincide. The 30-stock tech portfolio feels diversified and
    behaves like one large bet; a 12-asset portfolio across stocks, bonds, countries and
    styles is genuinely spread.
  </p>
  <p>
    A natural follow-up: how many holdings are enough? Empirical work going back to Evans
    and Archer (1968) and refined by Statman (1987) found the curve of risk reduction is
    steep at first and then flattens: going from 1 to 10 stocks removes a large share of
    idiosyncratic risk, 20 to 30 captures most of the rest, and beyond roughly 40 you are
    adding little but fees and tracking burden. For a US stock portfolio, the classic
    finding is that 30–40 names eliminate roughly 90 per cent of diversifiable risk; what
    remains is the market itself. Cheap index funds make this a solved problem — but the
    curve's steep early section is also why holding just three or four individual stocks
    is a category error rather than a strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Markowitz: portfolio construction as optimisation</h2>
  <p>
    Harry Markowitz's 1952 paper turned the free lunch into an algorithm: for any target
    return, compute the combination of assets with the lowest volatility (or for any risk,
    the highest return). Plot every attainable combination and you get the
    <strong>efficient frontier</strong> — the curve of portfolios that are not dominated by any
    other. Rational risk-taking means sitting on the frontier and choosing <em>where</em> on it
    according to your needs; anything inside the curve is donated risk. Markowitz won the
    1990 Nobel prize for it. Its practical limits are just as important: the optimiser needs
    <em>estimated</em> expected returns, volatilities and correlations, and it amplifies
    estimation error relentlessly — small errors in inputs produce wild over-concentration in
    whatever looked best historically ("error maximisation"). This is why practitioners use
    the theory qualitatively: spread across imperfectly correlated asset classes, don't chase
    the numerical optimum, and prefer robust simple splits over fragile clever ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Systematic and idiosyncratic risk — and what CAPM says you're paid for</h2>
  <p>
    Split any asset's risk in two. <strong>Idiosyncratic risk</strong> is specific to it — a
    drug trial fails, a CEO leaves — and it is <em>diversifiable</em>: spread across hundreds
    of holdings, it cancels by the √n law. <strong>Systematic risk</strong> moves with the
    whole market — recessions, rate shocks, wars — and no amount of diversification among
    stocks removes it. The <strong>CAPM</strong> (Sharpe, Lintner, 1960s) draws the consequence:
    the market pays a premium only for the risk you cannot diversify away. That risk is
    summarised in <strong>beta</strong>, an asset's sensitivity to market moves: a stock with
    beta 1.5 tends to move 15 per cent when the market moves 10. The model's pricing line:
    E[r] = r<sub>f</sub> + β × (E[r<sub>m</sub>] − r<sub>f</sub>) — the risk-free rate plus
    beta times the market premium. With a 2 per cent risk-free rate and an 8 per cent market
    expectation, beta 1.5 predicts an 11 per cent expected return. The punchline for any
    investor: <strong>the market pays nothing for concentrated risk you could have cancelled
    yourself</strong> — holding three stocks is not brave, it is uncompensated.
  </p>
  <p>
    CAPM is also the entry point to its own critique: decades of data show beta alone
    explains returns poorly. Size (small companies out-earn their beta), value (cheap stocks
    out-perform expensive ones) and momentum all persist as factors — the
    <strong>Fama–French</strong> line of work that reframed "risk" as exposure to multiple
    systematic drivers. CAPM survives as the first-order map: diversifiable risk earns zero,
    and the market's premium is the only free return on offer.
  </p>

  <h2>Building the real portfolio</h2>
  <p>
    The evidence ranks the decisions. Brinson, Hood and Beebower (1986) found that asset
    allocation policy — the split among stocks, bonds and cash — explained about
    <strong>90 per cent</strong> of the variation in typical fund returns over time; security
    selection and timing fight over the remainder. So the build order is: choose the
    stock/bond split (the risk dial; a 60/40 portfolio has historically endured half to
    two-thirds of equity drawdowns while smoothing the path), then diversify within each
    class (broad index funds: one S&amp;P 500 fund is 500 companies, a global fund
    thousands — pre-diversified at near-zero cost), then hold. <strong>Rebalancing</strong>
    maintains the design: if the 60/40 portfolio (60,000 in stocks, 40,000 in bonds of a
    100,000 total) sees stocks rise 25 per cent while bonds sit, the weights drift to
    65/35 — riskier than chosen. Rebalancing sells about 5,000 of stocks and buys bonds,
    mechanically selling high and buying low, and — more importantly — capping the
    <strong>risk drift</strong> that silently converts a balanced portfolio into a
    concentrated one. Annual or threshold-based rebalancing is enough; more is friction
    without benefit.
  </p>
  <p>
    One design principle runs through all real construction: <strong>the allocation should
    match the horizon</strong>. Money needed next year has no business in equities, however
    attractive their long-run record — sequence risk (being forced to sell into a crash)
    dominates over short spans. This is the logic of <strong>target-date funds</strong>,
    which run a <em>glide path</em>: roughly 90 per cent equities for a retirement twenty-five
    years out, stepping down toward 50–60 per cent as the date approaches, automatically.
    Whether the specific glide paths on offer are optimally shaped is debatable — but the
    underlying principle, that risk capacity declines as the spending date nears, is
    textbook portfolio construction.
  </p>

  <h2>When diversification fails</h2>
  <p>
    The free lunch comes with a footnote: <strong>correlations are state-dependent, and they
    spike in crises</strong>. In October 2008 nearly everything fell together — equities,
    credit, commodities, hedge strategies — because the shock hit funding itself; the
    √n law assumed independence that no longer existed. 2022 was the other stress case:
    inflation pushed rates up and stocks and bonds fell together, breaking the classic
    60/40 hedge. The correct reading is not "diversification is useless" but that it
    removes everyday risk, not <strong>tail risk</strong> — the possibility that all
    correlations converge to 1 at the worst moment. The practical mitigations: hold some
    assets whose crisis behaviour is structurally different (cash and short government
    bonds are the reliable ones; gold and trend-following strategies have partial
    records), keep the emergency buffer outside markets entirely, and treat any strategy
    that "never loses" with the suspicion its 2008 record deserves.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert extensions. <strong>International diversification</strong> halves
    single-country risk — yet investors everywhere exhibit <em>home bias</em>, holding
    mostly domestic assets; the case for global weightings (roughly, own the world market)
    is one of the strongest in the literature. <strong>Currency</strong> is a second axis:
    unhedged foreign bonds are part bond, part currency bet; equities are usually left
    unhedged because currency swings wash out over long horizons. <strong>Factor
    investing</strong> operationalises the Fama–French critique — tilting toward size,
    value and momentum exposures in cheap fund form — with the standing warning that
    factors underperform for years at a stretch, which is the price of their premium.
    And <strong>over-diversification</strong> ("diworsification", Peter Lynch's coinage) is
    real: past a few dozen overlapping holdings, incremental benefit flattens while fees,
    complexity and dilution of conviction grow. Two institutional responses to Markowitz's
    estimation problem are worth knowing. <strong>Risk parity</strong> abandons expected
    returns entirely and equalises each asset's <em>risk</em> contribution instead —
    because volatilities and correlations are far more stable through time than expected
    returns, the resulting portfolios (heavily weighted toward bonds, levered to restore
    return) proved remarkably robust through the 2000s, though 2022 exposed their hidden
    bet on the stock–bond correlation. The <strong>Black–Litterman</strong> model, from
    Goldman Sachs in 1992, attacks the error-maximisation problem from the other side: it
    starts from the equilibrium assumption that the market's own weights are fair, then
    lets an investor tilt modestly away with explicit confidence levels — producing
    allocations that move smoothly instead of lurching. The open question is generational: the
    60/40 portfolio was built on the stock–bond correlation being negative. Whether that
    holds in inflationary regimes — or whether the future belongs to broader allocations
    including real assets — is the live debate in every pension fund on Earth.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Markowitz (1952); Brinson, Hood &amp; Beebower (1986); Sharpe on CAPM; Fama &amp; French on factors; Bernstein, The Intelligent Asset Allocator.</p>
